1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
You know what, if the way you learn to describe space in, like, high school geometry is just fundamentally backwards.

2
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:10,380
It is a pretty wild thought to entertain.

3
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:12,560
Right. I mean, think back to those days for a second.

4
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:22,520
You had this piece of graph paper, and your teacher told you to draw a horizontal x-axis and a vertical y-axis, and suddenly, like, space was defined.

5
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:23,860
Yeah, the grid was just there.

6
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:30,380
Exactly. It felt so absolute right, like you were peeling back the curtain and revealing the true underlying grid of the universe.

7
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:32,860
That is exactly how we're conditioned to see it.

8
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:36,020
But what if a coordinate system isn't a map of reality at all?

9
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:47,160
What if it's more like a computational interface, like a software dashboard on your computer that we just actively choose to project onto reality just to make certain math easier?

10
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:52,640
That is a profoundly radical reframing of how we interact with the physical world.

11
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:59,840
I mean, we are so heavily conditioned by that graph paper to think of coordinates as innate properties of the space itself.

12
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:00,900
Right, like they're just built in.

13
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:07,600
Exactly. We look at a star or a molecule, and we say that point has coordinates, as if the numbers are physically attached to it.

14
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:08,680
Like a barcode or something.

15
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:09,380
Yeah, like a barcode.

16
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:17,580
But the reality, as this incredibly dense text we are exploring today argues, is that a coordinate system is purely a choice.

17
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:18,480
It is a lens.

18
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:28,420
And depending on which lens you pick, entirely different features of reality come into this sharp, brilliant focus, while others just become hopelessly mathematically distorted.

19
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:31,420
Which brings us to the actual source material.

20
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,220
Yes. So to you, our resident learner, welcome back.

21
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:41,420
Today we are immersing ourselves in a truly mind-bending deep dive into this 2026 undergraduate textbook.

22
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:45,160
It's titled Geometry as Computational Infrastructure.

23
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:47,480
Written by the independent researcher Flyxion.

24
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:53,260
Right, Flyxion. And our goal today is we are going to extract Flyxion's grand unified theory.

25
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:54,460
And it is grand.

26
00:01:54,620 --> 00:02:02,600
It really is. Because what this book proposes is that a single geometric shape, the sphere, and a single mathematical tool called the Laplacian,

27
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:06,300
they secretly govern entirely disconnected fields of science.

28
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:09,940
We are talking about the bioelectricity of regenerating flatworms.

29
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:13,660
Which is wild. And the architecture of irreversible computer code.

30
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:19,780
And even the literal mathematical geometry of how human beings distinguish between different ideas.

31
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,980
It is just a sweeping, almost audacious claim.

32
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:28,600
But what is truly fascinating here is how methodically Flyxion builds the case from the ground up.

33
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:33,620
You know, they don't just throw out a poetic metaphor about everything being a circle and expect you to just nod along.

34
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,460
No, they really bring the receipts.

35
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:42,620
They provide a rigorous mathematical framework, starting from the very foundation of what a coordinate system actually is.

36
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:49,480
And they prove how the mathematics of a spherical shell reappears organically in all these distinct domains.

37
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:50,680
Okay, let's unpack this.

38
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:53,240
Let's start right at that foundational premise.

39
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:54,860
The illusion of coordinates.

40
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:58,900
Because Flyxion hits this incredibly hard in chapter one.

41
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:06,580
They state that a coordinate chart is an interface between an observer and a space, not a commitment to the space's true shape.

42
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:11,640
So to make this concrete, let's talk about something as simple as a 2D circle on a piece of paper.

43
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:14,580
How does that prove the whole interface concept?

44
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:15,280
Gladly.

45
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:19,340
So let's visualize that standard circle drawn on a blank piece of paper.

46
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:19,740
Got it.

47
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:26,680
Now, if you want to describe a specific point on the edge of that circle mathematically, you generally have two common choices.

48
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,760
You could use a Cartesian chart.

49
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:32,200
Which is the standard X and Y grid right.

50
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:32,580
Right.

51
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:36,040
Measuring the horizontal and vertical distance from the center.

52
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:38,360
Or you could use a polar chart.

53
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:40,960
And polar throws out X and Y entirely right.

54
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:41,680
It does.

55
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:46,680
Instead, it assigns a radius, the straight line distance from the center, and an angle.

56
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:49,700
Basically, how far you've rotated around the center point.

57
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:49,960
Right.

58
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:55,940
And I remember in school, you just sort of switch between them based on whatever the textbook tells you to do for that specific problem.

59
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:58,740
It just felt like two different languages for the exact same thing.

60
00:03:58,800 --> 00:03:59,160
Exactly.

61
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,800
But the crucial insight is why we switch languages.

62
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:03,740
Why do both exist?

63
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:05,260
To make the math easier.

64
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:05,760
Yes.

65
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:09,980
Suppose you want to physically slide that circle across the paper.

66
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:10,940
A translation.

67
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:15,200
In Cartesian coordinates, the math for sliding is incredibly simple.

68
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:16,580
You just add to X or Y.

69
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:17,440
Exactly.

70
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:20,740
If you want to move it right, you just add a number to the X value.

71
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:22,520
You want to move it, you add to the Y value.

72
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:25,600
The whole shape moves neatly and linearly.

73
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:31,640
But what happens if you try to describe that exact same sliding motion using polar coordinates?

74
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:32,800
Oh, man.

75
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:34,320
I'm trying to picture it.

76
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:41,200
If I just slide the circle to the right, the distance from the original center to my point changes, and the angle changes, too.

77
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:43,200
It becomes a trigonometric nightmare.

78
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:51,460
The radius and the angle warp in incredibly complex, nonlinear ways, just to describe a simple slide across a desk.

79
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:53,040
It's just computationally exhausting.

80
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:53,960
It is.

81
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,980
But if you want to spin the circle...

82
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:56,160
Oh, right.

83
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:57,720
Then the script flips entirely.

84
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:58,400
Precisely.

85
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,400
Polar coordinates are perfect for rotation.

86
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:05,520
You just add a value to the angle, say, 45 degrees, and the radius stays exactly the same.

87
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,360
Math is trivial.

88
00:05:06,360 --> 00:05:13,460
Meanwhile, spinning a circle in Cartesian coordinates requires applying sines and cosines to both the X and Y values simultaneously.

89
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:15,360
It is messy and prone to error.

90
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:21,980
So the question Flyxion asks us to consider is, which coordinate system is the true description of the circle?

91
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:26,360
And the answer has to be neither right, because they're just tools.

92
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:27,160
Neither.

93
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:32,560
They are both just computational interfaces optimized for completely different tasks.

94
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:36,780
The Cartesian chart is a software tool built to make translation easy.

95
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:40,200
And the polar chart is a tool built to make rotation easy.

96
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:40,820
Exactly.

97
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:47,860
Two charts assigning different numerical labels to the same physical point are not disagreeing about the nature of reality.

98
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:50,780
They are just prioritizing different kinds of work.

99
00:05:50,980 --> 00:05:53,440
Which leads to a really dangerous trap, I think.

100
00:05:53,440 --> 00:06:04,660
Because if you forget that you are looking at reality through a highly specific optimized interface, you might mistake a glitch in the interface for a real physical feature of the universe.

101
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:05,320
Yes.

102
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:08,280
Flyxion has this great phrase for it, coordinate artifacts.

103
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:10,520
The cost of forgetting the interface.

104
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:15,020
And they use the Earth as an example here, which perfectly illustrates the danger.

105
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,660
Think about 3D spherical coordinates.

106
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:19,280
Longitude and latitude.

107
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:20,220
We use them every day.

108
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:24,320
Now imagine you are standing exactly on the geographic North Pole.

109
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:26,180
What is your longitude?

110
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:29,280
Well, longitude lines run from pole to pole.

111
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:32,840
So if I'm standing exactly on the North Pole, it doesn't exist.

112
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:33,100
Right.

113
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:36,220
I mean, I'm at the point where all the longitude lines crash into each other.

114
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:42,700
I could spin in a circle on my heel and theoretically pass through every single longitude on Earth in one second.

115
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:43,440
Exactly.

116
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:49,520
Mathematically, in that specific coordinate chart, the longitude at the pole is undefined.

117
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:51,280
It looks like a singularity.

118
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:54,500
Because multiple values exist at the same point simultaneously.

119
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:55,040
Right.

120
00:06:55,200 --> 00:07:01,680
Now, if you are a physicist or a meteorologist running a careless weather simulation and your math hits the North Pole,

121
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:07,140
your equations might literally blow up to infinity because they can't handle an undefined variable.

122
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:09,040
But this raises a vital question, right?

123
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:09,320
Yeah.

124
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:13,340
Is there a physical gaping hole in the Earth at the North Pole?

125
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,180
Is gravity collapsing there?

126
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:16,460
Is it?

127
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:16,980
No.

128
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:18,480
I mean, it's just ice.

129
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:20,980
The space is smooth and perfectly ordinary.

130
00:07:21,220 --> 00:07:24,540
The singularity is entirely a failure of our labeling scheme.

131
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:30,800
The latitude-longitude system we chose is simply incapable of assigning a single well-defined number

132
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:34,060
to a point that sits exactly on the axis of its own rotation.

133
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,380
So the map is broken, not the territory.

134
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:37,020
Exactly.

135
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:40,000
So Flyxion gives us a diagnostic rule to handle this.

136
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,000
They say, change the chart and see what survives.

137
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:43,440
A very elegant rule.

138
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:48,600
Meaning if the singularity disappears when you just use a different set of coordinates,

139
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:54,060
like, say, sending your maps equator through the poles instead, then the problem was never real.

140
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:56,000
It was an artifact of your math.

141
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,380
And if we connect this to the bigger picture of scientific research, it is a vital discipline.

142
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:05,940
Whether you are modeling fluid dynamics or black holes or stock markets,

143
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:10,840
you have to constantly ensure that the striking anomalous results you are getting

144
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:14,420
aren't just accidents of how you chose to format the data.

145
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:15,840
I love that rule.

146
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,940
It's incredibly empowering for a learner.

147
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:22,040
But I kind of want to push back a little here because this sounds almost too convenient.

148
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:22,960
How so?

149
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:27,800
Wait, so is every weird geometric glitch just a bad labeling scheme?

150
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:31,780
Like, if I'm an astronomer and I find something in space that was mathematically broken,

151
00:08:32,180 --> 00:08:36,140
can I always just assume I'm using the wrong coordinate interface, pat myself on the back

152
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:36,700
and wave it away?

153
00:08:36,860 --> 00:08:41,780
It is a very tempting thought to believe that the universe is perfectly smooth and only our

154
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:42,600
maps are flawed.

155
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:43,320
But no.

156
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:47,460
And Flyxion is extremely careful to address this exact point.

157
00:08:47,460 --> 00:08:51,480
They draw a hard mathematical line between artifacts of the chart,

158
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:56,020
like our North Pole example, and what topology calls invariance of the embedding.

159
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:57,820
Because sometimes the problem is real.

160
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:01,320
And they bring up this thing called the Alexander Horn Sphere to prove it.

161
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:06,420
Which, just the name alone, sounds like an esoteric heavy metal band.

162
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:08,820
What exactly is a horn sphere?

163
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:13,040
It is one of the most famous and unnerving mathematical constructs in topology.

164
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:18,680
It beautifully or perhaps terrifyingly illustrates the difference between a flat embedding where

165
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:22,900
things behave normally and a wild embedding where the universe itself is tangled.

166
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:24,060
Okay, I'm braced for this.

167
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:29,400
I want you to imagine a standard, perfectly round sphere in your mind, a rubber ball.

168
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:30,100
Got it.

169
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:35,720
We know it has a distinct inside and a distinct outside separated by the rubber skin.

170
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:40,580
Now imagine taking two spots on the outside of that rubber ball, pinching them, pulling

171
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:43,900
them out like two long horns and bringing them close together so they almost interlock.

172
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:46,220
Like two fingers just about to pinch each other.

173
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:46,820
Okay.

174
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:51,260
I am picturing a sphere with two intertwined horns.

175
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:52,000
Yes.

176
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,400
A little weird, but still manageable.

177
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:55,000
It hasn't broken my brain yet.

178
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:55,480
Give it a moment.

179
00:09:55,480 --> 00:10:01,840
Now on each of the tips of those two horns, grow two more smaller horns and have those

180
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:06,920
new pairs reach out and almost interlock and then do it again on the tips of those horns

181
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:09,300
and again, infinitely.

182
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:10,100
Wait, infinitely.

183
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:10,700
Infinitely.

184
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:17,560
You create this fractal, infinitely branching, relentlessly interlocking structure that gets

185
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:19,940
smaller and smaller but never actually touches itself.

186
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:22,020
This is the Alexander horn sphere.

187
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:22,520
Okay, yes.

188
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:23,980
That sounds like an absolute nightmare.

189
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:26,300
Here is the truly fascinating part.

190
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:30,220
From the perspective of an ant walking on the surface of that sphere, everything seems

191
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:31,000
perfectly normal.

192
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:31,500
Really?

193
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:32,060
Yes.

194
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:36,360
The local coordinate charts on the surface of the sphere itself can be made to look completely

195
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:36,980
ordinary.

196
00:10:37,580 --> 00:10:38,760
The ant can walk everywhere.

197
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:44,660
The surface is technically still a continuous, intact, unbroken 2D skin.

198
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:46,280
But it's infinitely wrinkled.

199
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:51,500
But the space around it, the 3D room you place this object into, has been permanently deformed

200
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:53,700
by this infinitely intertwined shape.

201
00:10:53,700 --> 00:10:56,900
Wait, how does an object deform the empty room it's sitting in?

202
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:00,760
Think about empty space as having its own topology.

203
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:07,560
Normally, if you tie a loop of string in the empty space outside a normal sphere, you can

204
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:11,900
always pull that string away and shrink it down to nothing without getting caught on the

205
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:12,180
sphere.

206
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:13,480
Right, it just slips right off.

207
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:19,620
But with the Alexander horn sphere, the exterior space is so deeply, fractally invaded by the

208
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:24,040
horns that you can tie a loop of string in the empty space around the horns and it gets

209
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:24,440
trapped.

210
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:27,940
Because it's caught in the infinitely branching labyrinth.

211
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:28,480
Exactly.

212
00:11:29,060 --> 00:11:31,720
The outside space is no longer simply connected.

213
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:34,840
The shape has literally tangled the void around it.

214
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:38,820
So unlike the North Pole, where you just change your map and the problem vanishes with the

215
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:42,280
horn sphere, the 3D space itself is genuinely compromised.

216
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:42,980
Exactly.

217
00:11:43,220 --> 00:11:49,520
You cannot just adopt a clever new 3D Cartesian or polar coordinate system to make the Alexander

218
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:53,020
horn sphere look like a normal smooth ball sitting in an empty room.

219
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:54,240
The pathology is real.

220
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:58,640
It is a real invariant feature of how that object is embedded in space.

221
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:00,500
It is a wild embedding.

222
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:02,460
No ambient change of coordinates.

223
00:12:03,220 --> 00:12:06,400
No clever dashboard UI can rescue it.

224
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:08,220
The map isn't broken here.

225
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:09,460
Reality is.

226
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:11,200
That distinction is so clarifying.

227
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:15,220
Changing the chart doesn't fix it if the universe is actually weird in that spot.

228
00:12:15,780 --> 00:12:21,080
So if we know that some spaces are wildly tangled, but most of the things we study aren't,

229
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:24,480
how do we choose the right map for the normal stuff?

230
00:12:24,680 --> 00:12:25,920
Yeah, that is the big question.

231
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:26,360
Right.

232
00:12:26,420 --> 00:12:30,960
Because if coordinates are just tools we choose to optimize our work and we want to analyze

233
00:12:30,960 --> 00:12:35,920
the most common forces in nature, how do we know which tool is the absolute best?

234
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:41,580
We saw Cartesian is great for sliding, polar is great for 2D spinning, but we live in the

235
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:42,200
3D world.

236
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:44,240
What happens when we level up to 3D?

237
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:47,280
This brings us to the core thesis of Flickshman's geometry.

238
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:52,280
They treat the selection of a coordinate system as a genuine, rigorous optimization problem.

239
00:12:52,740 --> 00:12:56,080
You don't just pick a coordinate system because it is traditional or because your teacher handed

240
00:12:56,080 --> 00:12:56,820
you graph paper.

241
00:12:57,080 --> 00:13:01,380
You pick it because it mathematically minimizes the computational work required to express

242
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:03,220
a specific class of transformations.

243
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:05,460
What mathematicians call group actions.

244
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:06,160
Correct.

245
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:11,500
Group actions are just formal ways of describing how things change or move symmetrically.

246
00:13:11,780 --> 00:13:15,640
We already covered translation sliding left, right, up, down, where the Cartesian grid

247
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:20,020
is king, then there is axial symmetry like the shape of a pipe or a cylinder.

248
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:25,500
If your physical system involves fluid spinning around inside a pipe or sliding down its length,

249
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,220
Flickshman points out that cylindrical coordinates are maximally adapted.

250
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:31,460
The math becomes almost effortless.

251
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:36,720
But the big one, the symmetry that this entire deep diet hinges on, is rotational symmetry around

252
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:39,240
a single central point in three dimensions.

253
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:39,780
Yes.

254
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:44,080
Things like gravity pulling toward a planet's core or an atom's nucleus or even a localized

255
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:45,700
explosion radiating outward.

256
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:49,620
The math term for this group of rotations is SO3, right?

257
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:50,080
Yes.

258
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:53,320
The special orthogonal group in three dimensions or SO3.

259
00:13:53,660 --> 00:13:59,240
When a physical or abstract system possesses that SO3 symmetry, when its dynamics are fundamentally

260
00:13:59,240 --> 00:14:04,440
organized around a single central point, Flickshman proves that the spherical coordinate

261
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:07,220
system is the unique maximal adaptation.

262
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:09,460
There is literally no better way to describe it.

263
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:10,820
There is no better way.

264
00:14:11,020 --> 00:14:13,380
In spherical coordinates, you have exactly three numbers.

265
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:18,940
One radial coordinate, your distance from the center, and two angular coordinates, essentially

266
00:14:18,940 --> 00:14:20,660
your longitude and latitude.

267
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:21,360
Okay.

268
00:14:21,780 --> 00:14:26,380
Under any 3D rotation, no matter how complex the spin, the radial coordinate stays exactly

269
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:26,880
the same.

270
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:32,580
The entire mathematical complexity of the rotation is absorbed purely by those two angular coordinates.

271
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:36,840
It concentrates the math exactly where it needs to be, leaving the radius untouched.

272
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:41,980
But Flickshman takes this way further than just the neat geometry trick for physics homework.

273
00:14:42,500 --> 00:14:47,320
They argue that whenever we use the spherical chart, we should read it through two completely

274
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:48,580
different conceptual lenses.

275
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:51,920
And missing them up is one of the biggest mistakes you can make.

276
00:14:51,920 --> 00:14:55,500
This is perhaps one of the most vital conceptual leaps in the entire text.

277
00:14:55,640 --> 00:14:57,300
It requires a total shift in perspective.

278
00:14:58,140 --> 00:15:03,360
Usually we think of coordinates as just a list of numbers XYZ or radius angle 1, angle 2.

279
00:15:03,520 --> 00:15:04,300
Just data points.

280
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:04,980
Right.

281
00:15:05,420 --> 00:15:10,840
But Flickshman insists that the radial coordinate and the angular coordinates represent qualitatively,

282
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:12,400
fundamentally different phenomena.

283
00:15:12,860 --> 00:15:14,180
They are not interchangeable.

284
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:19,860
Flickshman defines the radial coordinate, the distance from the center, as progression across

285
00:15:19,860 --> 00:15:20,740
levels.

286
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:22,100
Progression across levels.

287
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:26,800
Depending on what you are studying, this could mean physical depth, scalar magnitude

288
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:31,500
concentration, or even the level of irreversible commitment a system has made.

289
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:32,080
Okay.

290
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:33,360
Progression across levels.

291
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:34,300
And the two angles.

292
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:38,220
The two angular coordinates represent organization within a level.

293
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:43,980
This encompasses circulation, patterning, or reorganization that happens without changing

294
00:15:43,980 --> 00:15:46,320
which fundamental level the system occupies.

295
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:46,580
Right.

296
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:47,020
Okay.

297
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:50,640
So to make sure I'm really picturing this correctly, I was reading this chapter and thinking about

298
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,820
it like being at a massive multi-tiered music festival.

299
00:15:53,940 --> 00:15:54,620
I am intrigued.

300
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:55,080
Go on.

301
00:15:55,080 --> 00:16:00,720
So imagine you're at this gigantic outdoor festival and there are concentric rings of access

302
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:01,940
around the main stage.

303
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:07,400
You have general admission in the outer ring VIP, in the middle ring, and the all-access backstage

304
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:08,680
pass right at the center.

305
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:15,380
If I am moving, radially changing my distance to the stage, that means I am changing my fundamental

306
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:16,280
level of access.

307
00:16:17,100 --> 00:16:21,380
I am crossing security barriers, leveling up from general admission to VIP.

308
00:16:22,020 --> 00:16:23,480
That is progression across levels.

309
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:24,320
That's the radius.

310
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:24,820
I follow.

311
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:28,400
But the angular coordinates, that's just me walking around the perimeter checking out the

312
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:32,060
food trucks or the merch tents within my current VIP zone.

313
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:34,460
Radially, I am leveling up or down.

314
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:40,140
I'm angularly, I am just reorganizing my physical position on the exact same level of access.

315
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:44,940
That is a brilliant analogy and it captures exactly what Flyxion is warning against.

316
00:16:45,340 --> 00:16:49,560
Your movement toward the stage changes your fundamental relationship to the event.

317
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:52,020
Your privileges, your proximity, your access.

318
00:16:52,180 --> 00:16:53,340
It is a phase change.

319
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:53,660
Right.

320
00:16:53,740 --> 00:16:57,500
Your movement around the food trucks just changes your local context, whether you are near

321
00:16:57,500 --> 00:17:01,000
the tacos or the t-shirts, without altering your access level at all.

322
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:06,080
Flyxion argues that in scientific modeling, conflating the two, treating a fundamental change

323
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:11,160
in level as if it were just a lateral reorganization, is a devastating category error.

324
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:16,640
Because if I try to use a standard Cartesian XYZ grid at the music festival, I'm mixing up

325
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:19,160
my VIP upgrades with my food truck rents.

326
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:19,840
Exactly.

327
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:25,460
Moving 10 feet forward, a radial VIP upgrade, and moving 10 feet left, an angular walk to

328
00:17:25,460 --> 00:17:29,440
a food truck, both just look like adding a plus 10 to an X or Y axis.

329
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:35,840
The Cartesian map treats them as geometrically identical, but the spherical map separates them

330
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:36,280
perfectly.

331
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:37,020
Exactly.

332
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:40,320
The Cartesian grid is blind to the hierarchy of the system.

333
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:44,760
The spherical chart perfectly isolates the leveling up from the reorganizing.

334
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:49,340
And once you adopt this lens, you start to see why the sphere is so dominant in nature.

335
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:51,400
Which sets us up for something fascinating.

336
00:17:52,060 --> 00:17:56,160
Because if you stop moving radially, if you stay at exactly one VIP level, say 100 feet

337
00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:01,620
from the stage, and just walk the perimeter infinitely, you trace out a shell, a 2D sphere

338
00:18:01,620 --> 00:18:02,440
of fixed radius.

339
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:02,960
Correct.

340
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:04,740
And a shell naturally treats a boundary.

341
00:18:05,160 --> 00:18:08,260
It separates the VIPs on the inside from the general admission on the outside.

342
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:12,040
But Flyxion claims that boundaries in nature aren't just physical walls.

343
00:18:12,040 --> 00:18:14,640
They actually perform computational work.

344
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:15,300
Yes.

345
00:18:15,740 --> 00:18:19,100
We are shifting now into how these geometries dictate behavior.

346
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:20,640
You are absolutely right.

347
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:24,600
A fixed radius shell inherently separates an interior from an exterior.

348
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:31,140
But Flyxion draws on literature from way outside pure geometry, biology, statistics, computer

349
00:18:31,140 --> 00:18:36,740
science, to show that a boundary can mediate that separation in three structurally incompatible

350
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:37,160
ways.

351
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:38,540
Not all walls are the same.

352
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:40,500
Let's break down these three typologies.

353
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:43,540
The first one, Flyxion calls the synchronic screen.

354
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:46,860
And they specifically mention it's a Fristen-style boundary.

355
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:47,920
What does that mean?

356
00:18:48,180 --> 00:18:53,080
This is a direct reference to the theoretical neuroscientist Karl Frister and his concept

357
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:54,140
of a Markov blanket.

358
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:54,480
Okay.

359
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:57,600
Now, a Markov blanket is a statistical boundary.

360
00:18:58,260 --> 00:19:02,680
A synchronic screen is a boundary defined entirely in the present tense synchronic meaning

361
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:03,920
occurring at the same time.

362
00:19:04,180 --> 00:19:06,000
It acts as a statistical shield.

363
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:06,980
How does it shield?

364
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:11,720
Imagine a complex system like a cell or even a brain where the internal states, the parts

365
00:19:11,720 --> 00:19:14,920
on the inside, cannot physically see the external states on the outside directly.

366
00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:17,300
They can only perceive the blanket itself.

367
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:21,720
The boundary mediate perception and action based only on the current immediate moment.

368
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:26,600
So to build on the sensory deprivation idea, it's like a submarine's sonar screen.

369
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:27,120
Yes.

370
00:19:27,120 --> 00:19:31,940
The crew inside the sump, the internal states, cannot look out a window and physically see

371
00:19:31,940 --> 00:19:33,340
the ocean or the enemy ships.

372
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:33,720
Yeah.

373
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:38,520
They can only look at the glowing sonar screen, the boundary in the present moment.

374
00:19:39,060 --> 00:19:42,920
They make life or death decisions based on what that screen is showing them right now.

375
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:45,060
That is an excellent visualization.

376
00:19:45,060 --> 00:19:51,240
The synchronic screen evaluates the present joint state of the system, the pressure outside,

377
00:19:51,460 --> 00:19:56,020
the pings on the sonar, and mediates the flow of information inward and outward.

378
00:19:56,460 --> 00:19:59,420
But crucially, the screen itself does not remember the past.

379
00:19:59,740 --> 00:20:01,380
Doesn't know where the sub was yesterday.

380
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:01,920
Right.

381
00:20:02,020 --> 00:20:04,840
And it does not have a predetermined rigid goal for the future.

382
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:06,900
It is a purely reactive surface.

383
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:10,360
It just manages the now to keep the internal state stable.

384
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:10,640
Okay.

385
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:11,060
That makes sense.

386
00:20:11,120 --> 00:20:13,700
That's boundary number one, present tense, pure reaction.

387
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:18,080
But then Flyxion introduces the second type, the stored target boundary.

388
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:24,040
And this is where they bring in Michael Levin's flatworm experiments, which honestly blew my

389
00:20:24,040 --> 00:20:25,480
mind when I was reading the source material.

390
00:20:25,820 --> 00:20:27,580
Levin's work is paradigm shifting.

391
00:20:28,340 --> 00:20:34,200
If the Friston screen is the present tense, this boundary operates entirely in the future tense.

392
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:35,020
The future tense.

393
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:39,840
Michael Levin's research on developmental bioelectricity proves that boundaries in living

394
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:43,760
tissues aren't just passive walls where cellular growth happened to stop.

395
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:46,420
They are active computational layers.

396
00:20:47,220 --> 00:20:51,360
They are the visible trace of a shape being actively pursued by the system.

397
00:20:51,940 --> 00:20:56,880
The bioelectric gradient across the cells acts as a stored target morphology.

398
00:20:57,100 --> 00:20:58,860
Well, you've got to unpack that term for me.

399
00:20:59,160 --> 00:21:00,640
Stored target morphology.

400
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:02,700
It sounds like something a terminator would say.

401
00:21:03,180 --> 00:21:05,020
Morphology just means shape or structure.

402
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:09,580
So a stored target morphology means that the electrical boundary conditions of the tissue

403
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:13,800
literally contain the blueprint, the target for what the tissue is trying to become in

404
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:14,180
the future.

405
00:21:14,420 --> 00:21:17,760
And the experiment they cite in the text to prove this, it's wild.

406
00:21:18,120 --> 00:21:20,480
So Levin's team takes a planarian flatworm.

407
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:25,660
These worms are famous because if you cut them in half, the head grows a new tail and the

408
00:21:25,660 --> 00:21:26,500
tail grows a new head.

409
00:21:26,540 --> 00:21:26,800
Yes.

410
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:27,640
Incredible regenerators.

411
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:33,740
But in this experiment, before the worm heals, they alter the voltage, just the electrical

412
00:21:33,740 --> 00:21:35,140
gradient at the wound site.

413
00:21:35,460 --> 00:21:37,260
They don't touch the flatworm's DNA.

414
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:39,940
They don't add new stem cells or chemicals.

415
00:21:40,160 --> 00:21:43,060
They just manipulate the electrical boundary condition.

416
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:48,880
And the cells at the wound, using completely normal unaltered DNA, grow a second head instead

417
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:49,400
of a tail.

418
00:21:49,720 --> 00:21:53,060
It is a profound demonstration of how boundaries compute.

419
00:21:53,700 --> 00:21:55,340
Think of it like a construction site.

420
00:21:55,340 --> 00:22:00,120
The genome supplies the hardware, the bricks, the physical cells available to the tissue,

421
00:22:00,260 --> 00:22:01,500
the basic local rules.

422
00:22:02,160 --> 00:22:07,860
But the bioelectric boundary supplies the software target, the architect's blueprint that those

423
00:22:07,860 --> 00:22:09,000
cells are assembling toward.

424
00:22:09,340 --> 00:22:14,040
The boundary in Levin's flatworms is not just evaluating the present moment like our submarine

425
00:22:14,040 --> 00:22:14,500
sonar.

426
00:22:14,620 --> 00:22:15,560
It is an attractor.

427
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:20,080
It is actively pulling the biological system toward a specific future state.

428
00:22:20,480 --> 00:22:22,320
So the boundary dictates the future.

429
00:22:22,420 --> 00:22:23,560
That's our future tense boundary.

430
00:22:23,660 --> 00:22:24,360
What about the third one?

431
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:29,160
Flickhumman calls it the diachronic exclusion, diachronic meaning across time.

432
00:22:29,560 --> 00:22:32,460
And they reference something called an RSVP style boundary.

433
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:32,980
Yes.

434
00:22:33,480 --> 00:22:38,480
If the first is the present and the second is the future, this third boundary is strictly

435
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:39,680
defined by the past.

436
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:46,120
A diachronic exclusion is an accumulated historical record of what has been permanently, irreversibly

437
00:22:46,120 --> 00:22:46,680
ruled out.

438
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:50,680
It is a boundary constructed entirely out of rejected options.

439
00:22:50,860 --> 00:22:53,360
So it's not just a physical wall that says you can't go here.

440
00:22:53,540 --> 00:22:57,880
It's a conceptual wall built out of the fact that you already chose not to go there, and

441
00:22:57,880 --> 00:23:00,020
now that door is locked and bricked over forever.

442
00:23:00,340 --> 00:23:00,800
Precisely.

443
00:23:01,260 --> 00:23:07,000
Now regarding RSVP, the text uses RSVP as a framework for modeling these irreversible

444
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:07,420
commitments.

445
00:23:07,420 --> 00:23:12,160
Flyxion defines RSVP as regulated state veto protocols.

446
00:23:12,420 --> 00:23:12,900
Wait, really?

447
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:17,020
Because later in the text when they're talking about the fields, they list scalar-Lanfron

448
00:23:17,020 --> 00:23:19,100
entropy and vector S-L-E-V.

449
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:22,620
I was staring at the book thinking, Flyxion, buddy, that doesn't spell RSVP.

450
00:23:22,760 --> 00:23:23,980
A very sharp observation.

451
00:23:24,160 --> 00:23:25,880
The nomenclature is a bit tangled.

452
00:23:26,440 --> 00:23:31,100
RSVP, the regulated state veto protocol, is the overarching theoretical framework for how

453
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:32,740
a system handles permanent refusal.

454
00:23:32,740 --> 00:23:38,620
The four fields, scalar-Lanfron entropy vector, are the internal mathematical components of

455
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:39,120
that protocol.

456
00:23:39,520 --> 00:23:41,480
They are the mechanisms that enforce the veto.

457
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:42,500
Ah, okay.

458
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:43,880
That makes so much more sense.

459
00:23:43,960 --> 00:23:44,940
Thank you for clarifying that.

460
00:23:45,340 --> 00:23:48,820
So how does this RSVP veto actually build a boundary?

461
00:23:48,820 --> 00:23:54,980
In this view, when a system refuses an option, it doesn't just make that option less likely.

462
00:23:55,920 --> 00:24:00,060
A Fristin screen could handle less likely by just assigning a low probability to that

463
00:24:00,060 --> 00:24:01,260
path in the present moment.

464
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:07,280
But a diachronic exclusion permanently deletes that trajectory from the universe of options.

465
00:24:07,480 --> 00:24:08,040
It's gone.

466
00:24:08,300 --> 00:24:08,580
Gone.

467
00:24:08,580 --> 00:24:13,760
The boundary of the system is literally constituted by the history of barriers it has erected

468
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:15,260
against its own past choices.

469
00:24:15,580 --> 00:24:16,720
It is a boundary of history.

470
00:24:17,260 --> 00:24:20,620
So to recap, we have boundaries that mediate the present, like the sonar screen.

471
00:24:21,220 --> 00:24:24,200
Boundaries that pull toward a future target, like the flatworm blueprint.

472
00:24:24,720 --> 00:24:29,080
And boundaries built from past irreversible exclusions, like the RSVP history wall.

473
00:24:29,180 --> 00:24:29,360
Exactly.

474
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:32,480
Now here is where I really want to push back on Flyxion.

475
00:24:33,160 --> 00:24:35,480
Can't a boundary be all three at once?

476
00:24:35,600 --> 00:24:37,000
I mean, look at biological reality.

477
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:38,560
My cells have a cell wall.

478
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:43,120
That wall has a history of evolution and past cellular divisions.

479
00:24:43,460 --> 00:24:44,000
That's past.

480
00:24:44,400 --> 00:24:47,440
It is currently managing the temperature and chemical flow right now present.

481
00:24:47,860 --> 00:24:52,360
And it contains genetic and electrical targets for dividing into a new cell tomorrow future.

482
00:24:53,080 --> 00:24:57,460
How can Flyxion say that's mathematically impossible when it literally happens in my own

483
00:24:57,460 --> 00:24:57,800
body?

484
00:24:58,040 --> 00:24:59,800
It is a phenomenal challenge.

485
00:25:00,120 --> 00:25:04,220
And it goes right to the heart of the difference between biology and mathematical formalism.

486
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:10,780
You are entirely correct that a biological cell is a messy, pragmatic kludge of overlapping

487
00:25:10,780 --> 00:25:11,380
systems.

488
00:25:11,940 --> 00:25:15,360
Biology will stack a million different mechanisms on top of each other to survive.

489
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:15,580
Right.

490
00:25:15,660 --> 00:25:16,180
Whatever works.

491
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:16,860
Whatever works.

492
00:25:17,180 --> 00:25:20,080
But Flyxion is not describing a biological cell wall.

493
00:25:20,260 --> 00:25:25,160
